<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Colony of Saint Lucia</h1><p class="st-meta">1814 CE – 1979 CE · capital Castries</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">British colony on Saint Lucia after 1814, with French law and Kwéyòl culture, a coaling station for the Royal Navy and home of two Nobel laureates.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After about fourteen changes of ownership between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saint-lucia">Saint Lucia</a> was finally ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1814">Treaty of Paris</a> in 1814. The British kept much of the French legal system and Catholic church organisation that they found, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castries">Castries</a> remained the capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>As a crown colony <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saint-lucia">Saint Lucia</a> had no elected assembly for most of the 19th century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/custom-of-paris">Coutume de Paris</a> remained the basis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a> until a new code in 1879. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">Sugar</a> declined after emancipation; Indian indentured workers arrived from 1859. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castries">Castries</a> became a major coaling station for steamships from the 1880s. From 1838 the island belonged to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-windward-islands">British Windward Islands</a>. Bananas became the main export after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saint-lucia">Saint Lucia</a> joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/west-indies-federation">West Indies Federation</a>, became an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies-associated-states">Associated State</a> in 1967 and gained independence as Saint Lucia on 22 February 1979. The economist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-arthur-lewis">Arthur Lewis</a> and the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derek-walcott">Derek Walcott</a>, both born under colonial rule, later won <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prizes</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/colony-of-saint-lucia.jpg" alt="Colony of Saint Lucia" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Dunstan St Omer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Saint_Lucia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1814 CE – 1979 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Castries</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English and Saint Lucian Kwéyòl</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Saint Lucia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>British Saint Lucia</dd></dl></aside></div>
